╭─ STRIX ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│                                                                              │
│  Penetration test summary                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  # Executive Summary                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  A white-box assessment of the **test-vuln-hard** Flask application          │
│  identified **three confirmed injection vulnerabilities, all reachable       │
│  without authentication**. Every request parameter on every route flows      │
│  into an interpreter — either SQLite or the Jinja2 template engine —         │
│  without validation, parameter binding, or allowlisting.                     │
│                                                                              │
│  **Overall risk posture:** Elevated. The application executes arbitrary SQL  │
│  and arbitrary operating-system commands on behalf of any client that can    │
│  reach its port.                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  **Key findings**                                                            │
│  - **High — Server-side template injection leading to remote code            │
│  execution** on `POST /render`. Submitting a template expression as the      │
│  `tpl` form field executes arbitrary OS commands, with real command output   │
│  returned in the HTTP response. Three independent routes to code execution   │
│  were confirmed, so blocking individual template symbols is not a            │
│  remediation.                                                                │
│  - **Medium — SQL injection in the `ORDER BY` clause** of `GET /orders`. A   │
│  quote-free expression payload turns the response's row ordering into a      │
│  true/false signal; this was used to extract data the endpoint never         │
│  returns, including a marker value from the `note` column and the database   │
│  engine version.                                                             │
│  - **Medium — SQL injection in the `sku` field** of `POST /orders/by-sku`.   │
│  A quote in the JSON body breaks out of the SQL string literal; the          │
│  resulting `UNION` payload returned every row in the table regardless of     │
│  SKU and dumped the database schema.                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Business impact**                                                         │
│  - Confidentiality: any client able to reach the service can read the        │
│  entire database file and its schema, plus any configuration or environment  │
│  values reachable from the application process.                              │
│  - Integrity and availability: the command-execution primitive permits       │
│  modifying or deleting data and disrupting the service under the             │
│  application's user account.                                                 │
│  - Compliance: the same class of defect — user data reaching an interpreter  │
│  as code — accounts for all three findings, so a single remediation theme    │
│  addresses them together.                                                    │
│                                                                              │
│  The application binds to the loopback interface only, which limits who can  │
│  reach it and is why the two SQL findings are rated Medium rather than       │
│  High. That constraint is a deployment property, not a code-level control:   │
│  the code contains no defenses at all, so any reverse proxy, port forward,   │
│  or interface change exposes all three issues directly. The target is        │
│  described in its own source as a deliberately vulnerable lab harness,       │
│  which is consistent with the observed absence of every control.             │
│                                                                              │
│  # Methodology                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  **Engagement type:** White-box assessment of a local application codebase,  │
│  combining source review with dynamic validation against a locally started   │
│  instance of the application.                                                │
│                                                                              │
│  **Scope:** The Flask application and its SQLite datastore in the target     │
│  directory. Three endpoints were in scope for testing: `GET /orders`, `POST  │
│  /orders/by-sku`, and `POST /render`.                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Frameworks and approach:** Testing followed **OWASP WSTG** and **PTES**   │
│  practices, organized around a documented threat model that defined the      │
│  trust boundary (unauthenticated network client to route handler), the       │
│  attacker-controlled inputs (query parameter, JSON body field, form field),  │
│  and the invariant that must hold (user values reach SQL only as bound       │
│  parameters and templates only as data).                                     │
│                                                                              │
│  **Activities performed**                                                    │
│  - **Static triage:** an Opengrep pass using the bundled rule set, a         │
│  structural AST search, a secret scan, and a dependency and                  │
│  misconfiguration scan. Opengrep independently flagged both SQL sinks; the   │
│  secret and dependency scans were clean, confirming the exposure is          │
│  injection rather than leaked credentials. The large log artifact in the     │
│  directory was excluded from all scans.                                      │
│  - **Dynamic validation:** each endpoint was exercised against a locally     │
│  started instance. SQL findings were proven with payloads that extract data  │
│  the intended query cannot produce, and each was accompanied by a control    │
│  request demonstrating the expected baseline behavior. The                   │
│  template-injection finding was proven through to operating-system command   │
│  execution with real command output in the response.                         │
│  - **Engine verification:** the template engine was fingerprinted            │
│  empirically rather than trusted from documentation, which proved material   │
│  because the source's own docstring misidentifies it.                        │
│  - **Remediation verification:** each proposed code change was applied to a  │
│  copy and re-tested against the original payloads, with the unpatched        │
│  instance retained as a control to attribute the result to the change.       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Constraints:** The test environment could not write to the target         │
│  directory, so fixes were verified on copies rather than committed. One      │
│  endpoint could not be restarted end-to-end under its patched form because   │
│  a prior instance held the port; that limitation is stated in the relevant   │
│  finding's verification notes.                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  # Technical Analysis                                                        │
│                                                                              │
│  **Severity model:** Ratings reflect demonstrated impact calibrated against  │
│  the deployment constraint (loopback-only bind) rather than hypothetical     │
│  worst cases. Every impact metric corresponds to an observed effect, not an  │
│  inferred one.                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  **Confirmed findings**                                                      │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Server-side template injection to code execution — `POST /render`      │
│  (High, CVSS 8.8).** The handler passes the `tpl` form field directly to     │
│  `render_template_string`, which compiles its argument as template           │
│  *source*. Because Flask uses the standard, unsandboxed template             │
│  environment, expressions reach Python attribute chains and then the `os`    │
│  module. Command execution was demonstrated with real output returned in     │
│  the response body, using a unique marker command to exclude reflected or    │
│  coincidental output, and reproduced through three structurally independent  │
│  chains. Notably, the source docstring claims the engine is Mako; runtime    │
│  fingerprinting disproved this (Mako syntax was inert while Jinja2-specific  │
│  string-repetition syntax evaluated), which matters because remediation      │
│  guidance derived from the wrong engine would be misdirected.                │
│                                                                              │
│  2. **SQL injection in an `ORDER BY` clause — `GET /orders` (Medium, CVSS    │
│  6.2).** The `sort` parameter is concatenated into the statement with an     │
│  f-string. Because the value occupies a sort position rather than a          │
│  comparison, tautology payloads have nothing to break out of and constant    │
│  sub-selects leave ordering unchanged; a correlated conditional expression   │
│  bindable to a per-row secret was used instead, converting row order into a  │
│  boolean signal that was then binary-searched to recover column values the   │
│  endpoint never returns, plus the engine version.                            │
│                                                                              │
│  3. **SQL injection in a quoted `WHERE` predicate — `POST /orders/by-sku`    │
│  (Medium, CVSS 6.2).** The `sku` field from the JSON body is concatenated    │
│  inside single quotes. A quote in the body closes the literal and permits a  │
│  three-column `UNION` matched to the projected column list. Confirmed        │
│  effect: all rows returned for a request nominally scoped to one SKU, plus   │
│  a schema dump from the database metadata table. A boolean control           │
│  returning an empty set confirmed the input is parsed as SQL rather than     │
│  compared as a string.                                                       │
│                                                                              │
│  **Chaining assessment:** The three findings were evaluated for              │
│  combination. Each is independently sufficient at its own impact level, and  │
│  no combination produces a materially higher impact than the                 │
│  template-injection finding already demonstrates — the SQL findings read     │
│  the same database file the command-execution primitive can read directly.   │
│  The two SQL findings share a root cause (string building instead of bound   │
│  parameters) but sit at distinct sinks with distinct fixes, so they are      │
│  reported separately. No cross-finding chain was found that warrants a       │
│  separate composite finding.                                                 │
│                                                                              │
│  **Systemic root causes**                                                    │
│  - User-controlled data reaches interpreters as code in all three handlers;  │
│  there is no boundary at which values are converted to data.                 │
│  - Column and direction names from requests are treated as trusted SQL       │
│  structure, and the one place that requires an allowlist (sort ordering)     │
│  has none.                                                                   │
│  - No authentication, authorization, input validation, rate limiting, or     │
│  defensive middleware exists anywhere in the application.                    │
│  - Descriptive documentation does not match runtime behavior (the engine is  │
│  misidentified in source), which would misdirect anyone remediating from     │
│  the comments alone.                                                         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Assessment coverage:** All three runtime endpoints were assessed and all  │
│  three are reported above. Static triage, secret scanning, and dependency    │
│  scanning were completed with no additional findings; the non-runtime log    │
│  artifact was excluded from analysis by design.                              │
│                                                                              │
│  # Recommendations                                                           │
│                                                                              │
│  **Immediate**                                                               │
│                                                                              │
│  1. **Remove dynamic template compilation on `/render`.** Render a constant  │
│  template and pass the submitted value as a context variable so the engine   │
│  escapes it instead of compiling it. Corrections for the engine: do not      │
│  attempt to fix this with a denylist of template keywords or attribute       │
│  names — three independent paths to code execution were demonstrated, and    │
│  any name-based filter is bypassable. If user-authored templates are ever a  │
│  genuine product requirement, use a sandboxed template environment with a    │
│  strictly limited global and filter set, never the default environment.      │
│  2. **Parameterize the SKU lookup on `/orders/by-sku`.** Bind the request    │
│  value as a query parameter so the driver transmits it as data after the     │
│  statement is parsed. This is a one-line change with no API impact.          │
│  3. **Allowlist the sort key on `/orders`.** Since sort positions cannot be  │
│  parameterized, map the request value onto a fixed set of permitted column   │
│  identifiers and reject anything outside that set with a client error        │
│  rather than passing it to the database.                                     │
│  4. **Correct the source documentation** that misidentifies the template     │
│  engine. Remediation followed from the documentation would be wrong.         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Short-term**                                                              │
│                                                                              │
│  5. **Audit for the same pattern repository-wide:** every place a request    │
│  value is formatted into a query string, a shell command, a template, or a   │
│  path. Fix them as a class, not individually.                                │
│  6. **Add regression tests** asserting that quote, comment, `UNION`,         │
│  expression, and template-expression payloads cannot alter results or        │
│  execute, and that legitimate inputs continue to return their expected rows  │
│  and rendered output.                                                        │
│  7. **Apply least privilege to the datastore connection** and consider a     │
│  database authorizer that denies access to metadata tables and               │
│  non-application tables, reducing the blast radius of any future injection.  │
│                                                                              │
│  **Medium-term**                                                             │
│                                                                              │
│  8. **Make exposure limits explicit rather than incidental.** The loopback   │
│  bind currently limits reach; treat that as defence in depth and confirm     │
│  deployment intent. Any reverse-proxy or interface change exposes all three  │
│  issues directly, and severity rises accordingly.                            │
│  9. **Introduce a single input-handling convention** so new endpoints        │
│  cannot reintroduce the pattern: validated, typed, allowlisted parameters    │
│  at the boundary, with binding as the default rather than an option.         │
│                                                                              │
│  **Retest and validation:** Re-run the three proofs of concept after         │
│  remediation — the command-execution payloads must render as inert text,     │
│  the ordering payload must not change row order, and the `UNION` payload     │
│  must return an empty set while baseline requests keep working. Apply the    │
│  same validation to the repository-wide audit before closing the items.      │
│                                                                              │
│                                                                              │
